  "notes": "Kiowa-Tanoan (Northern Tiwa) language of Taos Pueblo, New Mexico, written in the practical orthography of Trager (1948). The letters are Americanist rather than IPA, so several carry values that do not match their IPA namesakes: <a> is /æ/, <o> is /ɑ/, <ə> is /ɤ/, and the ogonek letters <į ę ą ǫ ų> are the nasal vowels /ĩ ẽ æ̃ ɑ̃ ũ/. The nasal values are Trager's PHONEMIC ones; the Taos phonology article's vowel-allophony table gives the phonetic realisations [ĩ æ̃ ã ɔ̃ ũ], which this spec does not emit because the whole transcription is phonemic. Under Trager's 1948 analysis the aspirates, ejectives and labialised consonants are CLUSTERS, so <ph th> are /ph th/, <p' t' k' c'> are /pʔ tʔ kʔ tʃʔ/, <kw xw> are /kw xw/, and <phr> is /fɾ/ — the marginal labial fricative, which Trager 1946 wrote <fr>. Trager's earlier (1946) analysis treats the same segments as unit phonemes /pʰ tʰ pʼ tʼ kʼ tʃʼ kʷ xʷ/; a consumer that wants that notation can rewrite the clusters mechanically. <c> is /tʃ/, <ł> is /ɬ/, <y> is /j/, and <r> is /ɾ/ (Spanish loanwords only). Each vowel letter carries one of six diacritics encoding a stress+tone combination, which this spec resolves to the tone alone: acute and grave are MID tone (primary and secondary stress) and surface as a macron, double acute and caron are HIGH tone and surface as an acute, circumflex and double grave are LOW tone and surface as a grave, and an unmarked vowel is unstressed and toneless. The three-way stress distinction the same diacritics also encode is left unencoded: the mapping is grapheme-local and cannot place a word-level prominence mark. In a mid-tone vowel cluster (/ie iæ ĩẽ uɑ ɤɑ/) the macron is written on both components; under high and low tone only the first component carries the mark. The word-initial glottal stop is a phoneme of every vowel-initial word but is left unwritten in this orthography, so it is restored positionally.",
  "iso639_3": "twf",
  "glottolog_code": null,
