      "pages": null,
      "notes": "Carries the two tables this spec is built from: the Trager 1946 / Trager 1948 / IPA symbol correspondence (c=tʃ, ł=ɬ, r=ɾ, y=j, and the 1948 cluster readings ph, pʔ, tʔ, kʔ, tʃʔ, kw, xw), and the stress+tone table on the syllable 'ta' giving á=ˈtā, à=ˌtā, a̋=ˈtá, ǎ=ˌtá, â=ˈtà, ȁ=ˌtà, ta=unstressed. Its oral-vowel chart gives a=[æ], o=[ɑ], ə=[ɤ], and its prose notes that Trager's <o> is phonetically [ɑ]. It also reports, citing Trager (1948:158), that the glottal stop is not written word-initially in the practical orthography.",
      "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taos_language"
    },
    {
      "id": "twf_wikipedia_phonology",
      "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
      "year": 2024,
      "title": "Taos phonology",
      "publisher": "Wikimedia Foundation",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taos_phonology",
      "pages": null,
      "notes": "Worked orthography/phonemic pairs that fix the nasal vowels and the cluster tone spread: <kwę̀mų́na> = /ˌkʷẽ̄ˈmũ̄næ/ and <mę̀sotu’úna> = /ˌmẽ̄sɑtuˈʔūnæ/ give ę=/ẽ/; <mą́kuna> = /ˈmæ̃̄kunæ/ gives ą=/æ̃/; <hǫ́luma> = /ˈhɑ̃̄lumæ/ gives ǫ=/ɑ̃/; <wa̋mą> = /ˈwǽmæ̃/ confirms the double acute as high tone and <mą̂t’emą> = /ˈmæ̃̀tʼemæ̃/ the circumflex as low; <k’ə́onemą> = /ˈkʼɤ̄ɑ̄nemæ̃/, <cìatúną> = /ˌtʃīǣˈtūnæ̃/ and <thį̀ę’éna> = /ˌtʰĩ̄ẽ̄ˈʔēnæ/ show the mid-tone macron on both cluster components; <íałona> = /ˈʔīǣɬɑnæ/ and <ȍdénemą> = /ˌʔɑ̀ˈdēnemæ̃/ show the restored word-initial glottal stop. Its vowel-cluster section lists the five native clusters as /ie, iæ, ĩẽ, uɑ, ɤɑ/.",
      "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taos_phonology"
    },
